I always struggle with this question in  part because I there's gonna sound so  cliched but I'm very forward marching  and that can be debilitating at times I  feel like I've blinders on that are just  looking towards the future and sometimes  that affects me because it prevents me  from reflecting well on mistakes but the  first that comes to mind and this was  not just a mistake it was maybe a  collection of what I perceived at the  time to be disasters was in college  undergrad when I had a huge dispute with  my thesis advisor I also felt like I was  being funneled into one of two  industries management consulting or  Investment Banking this is at Princeton  very few industries come to recruit  there and as a competitor I was  compelled I felt compelled just as I had  in school to try to get a etc to compete  for the McKinsey's and Goldman Sachs of  the world and I remember getting through  one round of interviews and just  thinking to myself I don't want to do  either of these things but everyone else  wants them therefore perhaps I should  want them too and that's a big big  warning signal that's a red flag took me  a while to identify that and with the  help of an entrepreneurship high tech  entrepreneurship teacher named Ed Xiao  Zi SCH a you incredible man but due to  the issues with my thesis I wasn't going  to finish it on time for a host of  reasons if people want to learn more  about that they can look up some  thoughts on suicide pretty intense and  morbid title or some practical thoughts  on suicide and my name and it should pop  right up but I went into a deep  depression after leaving Princeton for a  year so I decided to take a year off to  do two things to work on the thesis and  I was told very surprisingly by my  adviser it better be the best thesis  I've ever seen in effect I'm going to  give you a terrible terrible grade and  there's a lot of background there I  won't bore people with right now but I  felt like I was completely trapped in a  corner with that and  on the positive side I decided to test  working at companies of different sizes  so I worked at Berlitz for a period of  time helping to redesign some of their  Japanese instruction curriculum and I  also worked at a political asylum  research firm and that was very small  two or three people and I learned a lot  so where it led me was realizing I  didn't want to work in a company of say  ten thousand people or many thousands of  people which Berlitz was and I was  working remotely so I also realized  there was a social component that was  really important to me I liked to work  around people even if my team were  remote I wanted to be surrounded by  people and ended up working at home by  myself a lot and it was mentally  destructive the political asylum  research was fantastic because it really  put my issues at that time into  perspective because I was dealing with  people who were let's just say  homosexuals in a country where they were  going to be killed or had been  threatened with death where family  members had been killed or for religious  differences and they were filing for  asylum in the United States to try to  escape this type of persecution and my  job was to find experts who could be  witnesses and determined what they would  call the meritorious nature of these  cases and brutal work because there were  instances where their case was valid but  for a host of technicalities or process  reasons in the u.s. system of addressing  such things we couldn't help them and so  literally be like sorry you have to go  back to this country where you're  probably going to be killed or your  family's going to be killed your kids  are going to be attacked and those very  very brutal but very helpful at the same  time for putting my first tool problems  should we say into perspective which are  no less serious in many respects I  should point out I don't fetishize that  like a lot of people and the the very  process of having to contend with these  existential issues seeing my classmates  graduate graduate a year before me  because I went back to school I felt  completely alone during this period of  year and it reinforced a few things for  me like the importance of exercise so  that is when I focused on boxing I went  to this gym in Trenton I don't know if  I've talked about this stuff before I  went to a gym in Trenton I was the only  white guy there almost everyone is on  work release and I remember the first  workout they're like oh see how you move  they put me in the ring with this guy  who is a heavyweight and immediately  broke my nose and just bled everywhere  and people laughed initially and then  they were like Jesus this is this this  guy's bleeding all over the ring and  pulled me out and I kept training there  and that from that point on they were  fine with me but the exercise is what  kept me sane and that's when I did the  the kickboxing training and so on that  led to the Nationals later in 1999  that's when that was and that story  ended up in the 4-hour workweek so a lot  of these existential issues led me to  make decisions then this is leading to a  better thing coming back into school and  continuing to study under edge shell and  look at entrepreneurship and starting my  own thing as the path as opposed to  having forged ahead perhaps looking back  on it trying to spin it with some silver  lining if my if I hadn't had the issues  in college that I did that led me to  drop out and have all of that pain  there's a very non-trivial chance let's  just call it 60 70 percent chance that I  would have ended up at McKinsey or  Goldman Sachs nothing wrong with those  things there are some people who are  just hard wired to succeed in those  environments and they're perfect fit but  you can't take an undergraduate body of  5,000 people and just divvy it up into  two industries and expect everyone to be  a good fit I wasn't but there's a very  good chance I would have ended up in one  of those career paths and I think I  would have been miserable I think  therefore would not have been terribly  good at it and I would have had ended up  changing direction five 10 20 years  later and instead the universe kicked me  in the nuts and uppercutted me in the  [ __ ] teeth and that was a good thing  so in the end of six months I figured  out what could have taken me 10 to 15  years  figure out so thanks for that universe I  don't need too many of those in my life  but at the time that was a good course  correction if you enjoyed this video I  have more to share all of the tools  tactics cool things that I'm finding  five bullet Friday it is an exclusive  newsletter it's completely free I sent  it out every Friday just has five bullet  points with the coolest most useful  things that I found or I'm revisiting  that week and you can find it by  clicking here takes about five minutes  to read super short very actionable 502  Friday also found at 4-hour workweek  comm board slash Friday
 

